":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"blind_id\":\"HOX050\",\"coverage\":[{\"point\":1,\"credit\":0.5,\"reason\":\"The answer says the order lets the fund hire or replace sub-advisers with board approval (including a majority of independent directors) under the order's conditions. It does not say that shareholders approve the structure first or that the prospectus discloses it.\"},{\"point\":2,\"credit\":1,\"reason\":\"It states the principal adviser 'remains fully responsible' for selecting, monitoring and supervising sub-advisers, which matches continuing oversight and ultimate responsibility.\"},{\"point\":3,\"credit\":0,\"reason\":\"It never says that an increase in the aggregate advisory fee rate still needs shareholder approval.\"},{\"point\":4,\"credit\":1,\"reason\":\"Optional point covered: 'Shareholders receive an information statement generally within 90 days after the new sub-adviser is engaged.'\"}],\"central_error\":null,\"local_error\":\"'Under a manager-of-managers exemptive order (or Rule 15a-5, where applicable)': Rule 15a-5 was only proposed (2003) and was withdrawn in 2009, so it is never applicable. This wrongly suggests a general rule could replace a fund-specific order. It is a material side falsehood but not the central error, because the answer still requires relief and board approval.\",\"grade\":\"partial\",\"rationale\":\"Required coverage totals 1.5 of 3, which is exactly half. Point 1 is only partly covered, point 2 is fully covered and the fee-increase point is missing. There is no central error, since the answer conditions changes on an order and board approval. The citation of the withdrawn Rule 15a-5 as possibly applicable is a material local falsehood, which would cap the grade at partial anyway. The affiliated-sub-adviser conditions are outside scope and were not scored.\"}","ttft_ms":4366,"type":"result","duration_ms":9521,"uuid":"bf682790-d6e3-4dfc-bfc7-0f4be0128d22","ttft_stream_ms":546,"time_to_request_ms":68,"first